# OCSP Monitoring Guide for Production Support

How to read `ocspcheck` output, what each number means, how to separate a slow
VA from a slow network, and how to build the trend dashboard from the JSON.

Written for the environment as found in the October 2026 investigation:
four EJBCA VAs (`pkivamwp01/02` in Ohio, `pkivaswc01/02` in SWC), one WLB name
`ngocsp.citi.com` that steers clients to their nearest pair by DNS with a 3 s
TTL, and a legacy responder `ocsp.citi.com` for the CA1 G2 PKI.

---

## 1. The pipeline

```
cron (every 5 min)                 MongoDB                      Streamlit
┌──────────────────────┐          ┌────────────────┐           ┌───────────────────┐
│ ocspcheck.py cert    │ ──json──►│ ocsp_checks    │ ──query──►│ trend charts      │
│   --servers ...json  │          │ one doc / run  │           │ per VA, per metric│
│   --ignore-aia       │          │                │           │ alerts            │
│   --no-crl --json    │          └────────────────┘           └───────────────────┘
└──────────────────────┘
```

Each run produces one JSON document with one entry per responder tested
(the WLB name plus each VA). Store the whole document; everything below is
derived from it.

Run it from **at least one box per site**. A probe in SWC sees MWP with a
35 ms round trip and SWC with 6 ms; a probe in Ohio sees the mirror image.
Only a local probe tells you how a pair looks to its own clients.

---

## 2. What one OCSP check actually costs

When a client (TIBCO, Windows, a browser) checks a certificate, it does this,
in order, and each step takes time:

```
 1. DNS      resolve ngocsp.citi.com ───────────────────► ~100 ms (3 s TTL, rarely cached)
 2. TCP      open a connection to the VA ───────────────► 1 round trip   (6 ms local, 35 ms cross-site)
 3. Send     the OCSP request ────────────────────────────┐
 4. VA       looks up the serial in its DB,                │ 1 round trip + VA processing
             asks the HSM to sign the answer ──────────────┤ (healthy VA: 50-100 ms of processing)
 5. Receive  the signed response ─────────────────────────┘
```

So a healthy check from next door costs about 100 (DNS) + 6 (TCP) + 6 + 80
(VA) ≈ 190 ms. From Ohio to SWC, about 100 + 35 + 35 + 80 ≈ 250 ms. Neither
is a problem. The problem on Oct 20 was step 4 taking 650 ms instead of 80.

---

## 3. The fields, and what each one isolates

Every entry in the `ocsp` array of the JSON has these. The point of having
them separately is that each one blames a different team.

| Field | Measures | If it's high, the problem is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `network.dns_ms` | time to resolve the hostname | the resolver / WLB nameserver — DNS team |
| `network.addresses` | which IP the name resolved to | (not a timing — tells you *which* VA a client got) |
| `network.tcp_ms` | one round trip to the VA (TCP handshake only) | the network path or distance — network team |
| `ms` | the whole OCSP exchange, connect to response | **see the formula below** |
| `status` | `good` / `revoked` / `unknown` / `unauthorized` / `timeout` / `tcp_error` / `dns_error` / `http_error` | configuration or availability — PKI team |
| `responder` | which OCSP signing key answered | tells you *which VA* answered via the WLB name |
| `this_update` / `next_update` | freshness window of the answer (10 min here) | PKI config |
| `checked_at` | timestamp of this specific check | the x-axis |

Top-level fields worth storing as indexed columns: `run_at`, `run_from`
(which probe box), `verdict`, `exit_code`.

---

## 4. The formula: separating the VA from the network

`ms` includes the network. To see how long the **VA itself** took:

```
va_processing_ms  ≈  ms  −  (2 × tcp_ms)
```

Why 2×: an OCSP exchange needs two round trips — one to open the TCP
connection, one to send the request and get the response. `tcp_ms` is one
round trip, measured separately, so subtracting two of them leaves the time
the VA spent thinking (DB lookup + HSM signature + JBoss overhead).

Worked examples from real runs:

| Node | `ms` | `tcp_ms` | `ms − 2×tcp_ms` | Reading |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| pkivaswc01 (local) | 95 | 11 | **73** | healthy |
| pkivamwp01 (Ohio) | 176 | 35 | **106** | healthy; the extra `ms` is distance |
| pkivamwp02 (Ohio) | 316 | 35 | **246** | watch — 2.5× its sibling |
| pkivamwp01 on Oct 20 (from pcap) | ~700 | 35 | **~630** | **sick** — this was the incident |

The lesson: `ms` alone would make Ohio look "slow" every day from SWC. The
formula makes the two sites comparable, and makes the Oct 20 number stand out
by a factor of 6 instead of 4.

For the WLB name (`ngocsp.citi.com`), there is one more piece:

```
client_experience_ms  ≈  dns_ms  +  ms
```

That is what a real client pays, because it has to resolve the name first.
With `dns_ms` ≈ 97 and `ms` ≈ 100, DNS is half the cost of a check. If
`dns_ms` spikes while every VA's `ms` stays flat, it is a DNS problem, not
a PKI problem.

