## 5. What to chart in Streamlit

Store every run; chart these. Time on the x-axis, one line per VA.

### Chart 1 — VA processing time per node (the main one)
```
y = ms − 2 × network.tcp_ms      one line per ocsp[].label
```
Flat lines around each node's baseline = healthy. One line lifting off while
the others stay flat = that node is sick. All lines lifting together = shared
dependency (HSM, DB, or the probe box itself).

### Chart 2 — raw `ms` per node
Same lines, no subtraction. Useful for "what did clients actually wait", and
for spotting when `tcp_ms` itself moves (a network change shifts this chart
but not chart 1).

### Chart 3 — `tcp_ms` per node
Should be two flat bands: ~6-11 ms for the local pair, ~35 ms for the remote
pair. Any wobble is network, not PKI.

### Chart 4 — `dns_ms` for the WLB name
Should sit around 100 ms. A step change is a resolver or WLB nameserver issue.

### Chart 5 — which VA the WLB name resolved to
Plot `network.addresses[0]` for the `load balancer` row as a categorical
strip over time. Shows the rotation (should alternate between the local
pair) and proves whether the WLB ever ejects a node.

### Chart 6 — status over time
A strip per node coloured by `status`. Anything other than `good` is an event.

### Table — latest run
One row per node with `status`, `ms`, `tcp_ms`, processing time, and the
change versus that node's 24-hour median. This is the "is anything wrong
right now" view.

