## 9. Mongo document shape

Store the `ocspcheck` JSON as-is, one document per run. Suggested indexes:

```
{ run_at: 1 }
{ run_from: 1, run_at: 1 }
{ "ocsp.label": 1, run_at: 1 }
```

For the charts you'll unwind `ocsp` into one row per (run, node):

```python
rows = []
for doc in coll.find({"run_at": {"$gte": since}}):
    for o in doc["ocsp"]:
        net = o.get("network") or {}
        rows.append({
            "t":        doc["run_at"],
            "probe":    doc["run_from"],
            "node":     o["label"],
            "status":   o["status"],
            "ms":       o["ms"],
            "tcp_ms":   net.get("tcp_ms"),
            "dns_ms":   net.get("dns_ms"),
            "ip":       (net.get("addresses") or [None])[0],
            "proc_ms":  (o["ms"] - 2 * net["tcp_ms"]) if o["ms"] is not None and net.get("tcp_ms") is not None else None,
            "responder": o.get("responder"),
        })
df = pd.DataFrame(rows)
```

Then chart 1 is `df.pivot(index="t", columns="node", values="proc_ms")` and
the baseline is `df.groupby("node")["proc_ms"].rolling("24h").median()`.

Keep `probe` as a filter in the app: the same node will have different
`tcp_ms` from each site, so never mix probes in one baseline.

